import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0067: Chunk Certificates","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0067-chunk-certificates.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0067-chunk-certificates.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0067-chunk-certificates.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0067-chunk-certificates" tabindex="-1">ADR-0067: Chunk Certificates <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0067-chunk-certificates" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0067: Chunk Certificates”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Proposed — deferred until untrusted chunk sources are a real concern.</p><p>Depends on <a href="./0066-dependency-graph-chunking.html">ADR-0066 — Dependency-Graph Chunking</a> (chunks are the unit being certified) and <a href="./0065-derived-artifact-cache-and-compression.html">ADR-0065 — Derived-Artifact Cache and Compression</a> (cached artifacts are the trust targets). Refines <a href="./0046-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level.html">ADR-0046 — Capability Discipline at the Language Level</a> (static capability verification) and <a href="./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.html">ADR-0044 — Language as Data and Runtime Split</a> (<code>Language</code> as serializable data).</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><h3 id="this-is-a-trust-provenance-feature-not-an-optimization" tabindex="-1">This is a trust/provenance feature, not an optimization <a class="header-anchor" href="#this-is-a-trust-provenance-feature-not-an-optimization" aria-label="Permalink to “This is a trust/provenance feature, not an optimization”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0065 (cache/compression) and ADR-0066 (chunking) are optimization techniques. This ADR is a different concern: <strong>trust</strong>. It answers &quot;do I believe this chunk is what its author claims it is, and does it come from a source I trust?&quot;</p><p>The distinction matters because bundling trust under &quot;optimization&quot; obscures the threat model. Optimization is about performance; trust is about safety. They share a unit (the chunk, per ADR-0066) but they have different failure modes, different stakeholders, and different design constraints. This ADR keeps them separate.</p><h3 id="the-threat-model" tabindex="-1">The threat model <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-threat-model" aria-label="Permalink to “The threat model”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0046 made capability discipline static: a <code>Language</code>&#39;s capability set is computed from its data, and <code>eval</code> checks it at the boundary. This prevents a <code>Language</code> from exercising capabilities it doesn&#39;t declare. It does <strong>not</strong> prevent:</p><ol><li><strong>Tampering.</strong> A chunk is modified after authoring (by an attacker, a corrupted cache, a MITM on a network load) to declare fewer capabilities than it actually exercises, or to replace a safe fragment with a malicious one.</li><li><strong>Misattribution.</strong> A chunk claims to be from a trusted author but isn&#39;t.</li><li><strong>Supply-chain substitution.</strong> A chunk dependency (ADR-0066 §2 <code>dependencies: Set&lt;ChunkId&gt;</code>) is replaced with a different chunk that has the same id but different content.</li></ol><p>ADR-0046&#39;s capability check inspects the <code>Language</code> data as presented. If the data has been tampered with to lie about its capabilities, the check passes falsely. Certificates close this gap by verifying <strong>provenance and integrity</strong>, not just declared capabilities.</p><h3 id="why-this-depends-on-adr-0066" tabindex="-1">Why this depends on ADR-0066 <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-depends-on-adr-0066" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this depends on ADR-0066”">​</a></h3><p>Chunks (ADR-0066) are the unit of certification. A certificate covers a chunk: its content, its dependencies, its author, its capability set. Without chunking, the unit is the whole <code>Language</code> — too coarse for incremental trust (one changed fragment re-certifies the entire language). With chunking, trust is per-chunk: a new chunk from a trusted author is certified independently; an untrusted chunk is rejected without loading the rest.</p><h3 id="why-this-is-deferred" tabindex="-1">Why this is deferred <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-is-deferred" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this is deferred”">​</a></h3><p>Today, all <code>Language</code> data is authored in-repo and embedded at compile time (ADR-0044 §6). There are no untrusted sources. Certificates add overhead (signature verification on every load, key management, revocation) with no benefit when the source is trusted by construction.</p><p>Certificates pay off when:</p><ul><li><strong>Mods/plugins from third parties.</strong> A user loads a mod&#39;s <code>Language</code> chunks; the host verifies the mod author&#39;s signature before eval.</li><li><strong>Network-loaded languages.</strong> Chunks fetched over a network can be tampered with in transit; signatures verify integrity.</li><li><strong>Shared caches (ADR-0065 §5 <code>NetworkCache</code>).</strong> A cache entry from another host must be verified before use — the cache is not trusted.</li><li><strong>Compliance/regulatory environments.</strong> A deployment requires provenance attestation for all loaded code.</li></ul><p>None of these drivers exist today. This ADR records the design — because trust is a distinct concern from optimization and should not be designed ad hoc when the first untrusted source appears — but marks it <strong>deferred until a concrete driver exists</strong>.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-a-certificate-is-a-signed-statement-about-a-chunk" tabindex="-1">1. A certificate is a signed statement about a chunk <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-a-certificate-is-a-signed-statement-about-a-chunk" aria-label="Permalink to “1. A certificate is a signed statement about a chunk”">​</a></h3><p>A certificate is a detached signature over a canonical serialization of:</p><ul><li>The chunk&#39;s content hash (BLAKE3, per ADR-0065 §2).</li><li>The chunk&#39;s dependencies (chunk ids + content hashes, per ADR-0066 §2).</li><li>The chunk&#39;s declared capability set (per ADR-0046 §1 <code>CapabilitySet</code>).</li><li>The chunk&#39;s <code>replaces</code> targets (per ADR-0044 §5, ADR-0066 §2).</li><li>The chunk&#39;s <code>scheme_version</code> (per ADR-0044 §7).</li><li>Metadata: author id, timestamp, certificate version.</li></ul><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Certificate = {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    chunk_hash: Blake3,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    dependencies: Map&lt;ChunkId, Blake3&gt;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    capabilities: CapabilitySet,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    replaces: Map&lt;FragmentId, ChunkId&gt;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    scheme_version: String,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    author: AuthorId,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    timestamp: u64,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    cert_version: u32,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    signature: Signature,  // over canonical_serialize of all above fields</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The signature is Ed25519. The author&#39;s public key is distributed out of band (key server, pinned in host config, or Web-of-Trust).</p><h3 id="_2-verification-happens-before-load-not-after" tabindex="-1">2. Verification happens before load, not after <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-verification-happens-before-load-not-after" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Verification happens before load, not after”">​</a></h3><p>A chunk is verified <strong>before</strong> it is loaded into memory. The runtime:</p><ol><li>Reads the chunk and its certificate.</li><li>Recomputes the chunk&#39;s content hash; verifies it matches the certificate.</li><li>Verifies the signature against the author&#39;s public key.</li><li>Checks the author is in the host&#39;s trust store.</li><li>Checks the declared capability set against the host&#39;s policy (ADR-0046 §3).</li><li>Recursively verifies each dependency chunk&#39;s certificate.</li></ol><p>If any step fails, the chunk is rejected. It is not loaded, not decompressed, not typechecked. This is defense-in-depth: ADR-0046&#39;s capability check inspects the data; this ADR&#39;s certificate verifies the data hasn&#39;t been tampered with and comes from a trusted source.</p><h3 id="_3-dependency-certificates-are-required" tabindex="-1">3. Dependency certificates are required <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-dependency-certificates-are-required" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Dependency certificates are required”">​</a></h3><p>A chunk&#39;s certificate includes its dependencies&#39; content hashes (ADR-0066 §2). When loading a chunk, the runtime verifies that each dependency&#39;s actual content hash matches the hash in the certificate. This prevents supply-chain substitution: a dependency chunk with the same id but different content fails verification.</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>for dep_id, expected_hash in cert.dependencies:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    dep_chunk = load(dep_id)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    assert(blake3(dep_chunk) == expected_hash)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    verify_certificate(dep_chunk)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>This is recursive: a chunk is only trusted if its entire transitive dependency closure is trusted.</p><h3 id="_4-trust-store-is-a-host-config-not-a-language-property" tabindex="-1">4. Trust store is a host config, not a language property <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-trust-store-is-a-host-config-not-a-language-property" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Trust store is a host config, not a language property”">​</a></h3><p>The set of trusted authors (public keys) is a host-level configuration, not part of the <code>Language</code> data. A <code>Language</code> does not declare who is allowed to author it; the host decides what to trust. This keeps trust out of the data model (ADR-0044: <code>Language</code> is pure data, no policy) and in the host&#39;s control (ADR-0046: the host attaches policies at registration time).</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span># host config</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>[trust]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>trusted_authors = [&quot;ed25519:abc123...&quot;, &quot;ed25519:def456...&quot;]</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="_5-revocation-via-a-revocation-list" tabindex="-1">5. Revocation via a revocation list <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-revocation-via-a-revocation-list" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Revocation via a revocation list”">​</a></h3><p>A certificate can be revoked before its natural expiry. The host maintains a revocation list (chunk hashes or author ids that are no longer trusted). A chunk whose certificate is on the revocation list is rejected, even if the signature is valid.</p><p>The revocation list is a host config, updated out of band (e.g., fetched from a key server periodically). This is the standard PKI pattern; no novel design here.</p><h3 id="_6-certificates-are-optional-per-chunk" tabindex="-1">6. Certificates are optional per-chunk <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-certificates-are-optional-per-chunk" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Certificates are optional per-chunk”">​</a></h3><p>Not every chunk needs a certificate. A chunk from a trusted local source (in-repo, compile-time-embedded) does not need one. A chunk from an untrusted source (third-party mod, network load, shared cache) does.</p><p>The runtime config specifies the policy:</p><div class="language-toml"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">[</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">certificates</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">required </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">always</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFAA80;--shiki-dark-text-decoration:underline;--shiki-dark-font-style:inherit;--shiki-light:#B31D28;--shiki-light-text-decoration:inherit;--shiki-light-font-style:italic;"> | &quot;untrusted-only&quot; | &quot;never&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><code>always</code> — every chunk must have a valid certificate (strictest).</li><li><code>untrusted-only</code> — chunks from trusted sources (local, compile-time) skip verification; chunks from untrusted sources (network, third-party) require it.</li><li><code>never</code> — no verification (default today; equivalent to no certificate subsystem).</li></ul><h3 id="_7-capability-set-in-the-certificate-is-a-claim-verified-by-adr-0046" tabindex="-1">7. Capability set in the certificate is a claim, verified by ADR-0046 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-capability-set-in-the-certificate-is-a-claim-verified-by-adr-0046" aria-label="Permalink to “7. Capability set in the certificate is a claim, verified by ADR-0046”">​</a></h3><p>The certificate includes the chunk&#39;s declared <code>CapabilitySet</code> (ADR-0046 §1). This is a <strong>claim</strong> by the author. The runtime verifies the claim by computing the <code>CapabilitySet</code> from the chunk&#39;s data (ADR-0046 §1 <code>CapabilitySet::from_language</code>) and comparing. A mismatch — the certificate claims <code>fs-write</code> is not used, but the data contains a <code>Foreign(&quot;fs-write&quot;)</code> scheme — is a verification failure. The chunk is rejected.</p><p>This closes the tampering gap: an attacker cannot modify the chunk to add a <code>Foreign</code> scheme without invalidating the signature, and cannot lie about the capability set without the runtime detecting the mismatch.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Provenance and integrity, not just declared capabilities.</strong> ADR-0046 checks what the data claims; this ADR verifies the data hasn&#39;t been tampered with and comes from a trusted author. Together they form defense-in-depth.</li><li><strong>Per-chunk trust.</strong> A new chunk from a trusted author is certified independently; an untrusted chunk is rejected without loading the rest. This composes with ADR-0066&#39;s lazy load.</li><li><strong>Supply-chain substitution is detected.</strong> A dependency chunk with the same id but different content fails the hash check in the parent&#39;s certificate.</li><li><strong>Capability-set tampering is detected.</strong> The certificate&#39;s declared capability set is verified against the data; a mismatch is a rejection.</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Cryptographic overhead on every load of a certified chunk.</strong> Ed25519 verification is fast (~100µs) but non-zero. For a chunk loaded on every eval, this adds up. Mitigation: verify once, cache the verification result alongside the chunk (ADR-0065 cache stores &quot;verified&quot; flag).</li><li><strong>Key management is a real operational burden.</strong> Key distribution, rotation, revocation, trust store management — this is PKI, not a wrapper. The host operator bears this cost.</li><li><strong>Revocation lists can grow.</strong> A long-lived deployment accumulates revoked chunks. The list must be pruned or bounded.</li><li><strong>A compromised author key compromises all chunks signed by it.</strong> Standard PKI failure mode; revocation is the mitigation, but there is a window between compromise and revocation.</li></ul><h3 id="neutral" tabindex="-1">Neutral <a class="header-anchor" href="#neutral" aria-label="Permalink to “Neutral”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Deferred.</strong> This ADR records the design but does not mandate implementation. A concrete driver (third-party mods, network-loaded languages, shared caches, compliance requirements) must appear before implementation begins.</li><li><strong>Separate from optimization.</strong> Certificates are not a performance feature. They are a trust feature that happens to share a unit (chunk) with the optimization features (ADR-0065, ADR-0066).</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-phases-deferred" tabindex="-1">Implementation phases (deferred) <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-phases-deferred" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation phases (deferred)”">​</a></h2><h3 id="phase-1-—-certificate-format-and-signing" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — Certificate format and signing <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-certificate-format-and-signing" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — Certificate format and signing”">​</a></h3><p>Define the <code>Certificate</code> struct, canonical serialization, and Ed25519 signing/verification. Test: sign a chunk, verify the signature; tamper the chunk, verification fails.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-trust-store-and-verification-pipeline" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — Trust store and verification pipeline <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-trust-store-and-verification-pipeline" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — Trust store and verification pipeline”">​</a></h3><p>Implement the trust store (host config), the verification pipeline (hash → signature → author → capabilities → dependencies), and the revocation list. Test: a chunk from a trusted author loads; a chunk from an untrusted author is rejected; a revoked chunk is rejected.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-integration-with-chunk-loading-adr-0066" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — Integration with chunk loading (ADR-0066) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-integration-with-chunk-loading-adr-0066" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — Integration with chunk loading (ADR-0066)”">​</a></h3><p>Wire certificate verification into the chunk-load path (ADR-0066 §3). A chunk is verified before decompression and typecheck. Test: the lazy-load pipeline verifies each chunk on demand.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-capability-set-mismatch-detection" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — Capability-set mismatch detection <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-capability-set-mismatch-detection" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — Capability-set mismatch detection”">​</a></h3><p>Verify the certificate&#39;s declared <code>CapabilitySet</code> against the computed one (ADR-0046 §1). Test: a chunk whose data contains a <code>Foreign</code> not in the certificate&#39;s capability set is rejected.</p><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0066-dependency-graph-chunking.html">ADR-0066</a> — Dependency-Graph Chunking (chunks are the unit of certification)</li><li><a href="./0065-derived-artifact-cache-and-compression.html">ADR-0065</a> — Derived-Artifact Cache and Compression (cached artifacts are trust targets; verification result can be cached)</li><li><a href="./0046-capability-discipline-at-the-language-level.html">ADR-0046</a> — Capability Discipline (static capability check; this ADR verifies the data the check inspects)</li><li><a href="./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.html">ADR-0044</a> — Language as Data and Runtime Split (<code>Language</code> is serializable data; certificates sign that data)</li><li>Ed25519 (RFC 8032) — the signature scheme</li><li>Standard PKI patterns (X.509, TUF, Sigstore) — key distribution, revocation, trust store design</li></ul>`,65)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};